<h1> Backend Scalability </h1>



In general, the back‑end scalability of a web application refers primarily to compute scalability and data‑layer scalability. Compute scalability can be achieved either horizontally or vertically. Because the topic is broad, this demonstration focuses specifically on horizontal scaling, in which additional instances are provisioned to handle increased request volume and prevent performance bottlenecks.

I begin by reviewing the capacity‑assessment process, using HTTP requests per second as an indicator of a worker’s processing capability. I then examine the logic and rules that govern automatic scaling in response to rising HTTP traffic.

At a later time, I will illustrate the horizontal scaling capabilities of Azure workers operating within the Azure App Service model Or Azure Container App model. I am also open to other vendors to explore. The current environment consists of Azure infrastructure and render.com. 

This website is for demonstration purposes only.

